# https://medium.com/@qk7b/short-firebase-how-to-deal-with-firebase-cloud-storage-access-rights-urls-and-cache-785bd57296fe
# Upload the document to Cloud Storage and allow others to access it.
Let’s hit the final stage of our journey, upload documents to **Cloud Storage** and make them accessible.

The thing is, **Cloud Storage** has the same kind of rules **Cloud Firestore** does.

rules_version = ‘2’;service firebase.storage { match /b/{bucket}/o { match /documents/{documentId} { // Helper function that, given a documentId (matching the rule) // Will look in CloudFirestore for the matching document, check for // the users map property the presence of the id of the user making the // request function hasDocumentReadAccess() { return request.auth.uid != null && firestore.get( /databases/(default)/documents/documents/$(documentId) ).data.users[request.auth.uid] != null } // Same but checks that the value is "owner" function hasDocumentWriteAccess() { return request.auth.uid != null && firestore.get( /databases/(default)/documents/documents/$(documentId) ).data.users[request.auth.uid] == "owner" } // Allow file creation if user has document write access allow create, write: if hasDocumentWriteAccess(); // Allow read if user has document read access allow read: if hasDocumentReadAccess(); } } // For everything else, deny match /{allPaths=**} { allow read, write: if false; }}The rules are key to what we are doing. The power of **Cloud Storage** rules is that it can rely on Cloud Firestore to determine things.

*Meaning you can access your database from the storage to check for rules.*

This way we can:

- Allow users to create a document with a specific id if the same id is found in **Cloud Firestore**
- Allow users to access this document (read) if the **Cloud Firestore** matching document contains their id

And all pieces of the puzzle fit together, when a user wants to upload an image.

- First, we create a **document **in **Cloud Firestore** in `documents/{documentId}` . This document contains a property `users `which is a map that contains `userIds` in keys and `owner|reade` in values.
- The we upload the document in **Cloud Storage** following the path `documents/{documentId}` (the same as in **Cloud Firestore** but it is not mandatory).
- The rules come in action, it looks for the user trying to upload the document and its id, checks in **Cloud Firestore** if this document exists and if the uploader has the role “owner”, if so, allows the creation, else denies.

## Now what about downloading ?
Remember our documents collection in Cloud Firestore ? Yeah let’s use that.

We know the document path, as it will be `documents/{documentId}` so it is easy, but if it is more complicated, we need to store the document location.

To do so, we will update the data in our Cloud Firestore document to add the mediaPath, from this

{ "users": { "VoEKLudN89ui8FvKqVzRtqTjwl04": "owner", "dLgqm1yZ576YWZdhbfd8pjNTvzH2": "reader" }}to this

{ "mediaPath": "/documents/abcdef", "users": { "VoEKLudN89ui8FvKqVzRtqTjwl04": "owner", "dLgqm1yZ576YWZdhbfd8pjNTvzH2": "reader" }}This way if the collection moves, or our document location moves, it still works.

Then all we need to do to display it is download it.

The easy way was to do it with urls. When I searches for ways, all I could find was people telling me to use `[getDownloadUrl](https://firebase.google.com/docs/storage/web/download-files#download_data_via_url)` or `[getSignedUrl](https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls)` once the document is uploaded.

But this was not what I was looking for…

- `getDownloadUrl` generates a long term url to allow access to the document, authenticated or not, once the url is public, anyone can access the file. That was not what I wanted, I want only authorized users to access my document
- `getSignedUrl` generates a temporary (you choose) url with specific roles… But same thing here, once the url is live, anyone can access my resource.

So I needed another thing, this is the `allow read` rule. I wont need an url, I will request the document directly from the **Cloud Storage** instance.

In `Flutter` it will be done this way:

final imageFuture = FirebaseStorage .instance .ref(documentPath) .getData();This method returns a `Future<Uint8List?>` which is my `Image` data. To display it all I need is :

return FutureBuilder( future: imageFuture, builder: (context, snapshot) { if (snapshot.hasData && snapshot.data != null) { return Image.memory(snapshot.data!); } else if (snapshot.hasError || snapshot.data == null) { debugPrint(snapshot.error.toString()); return Text(&#x27;Oppsie doopsie&#x27;); } return const CircularProgressIndicator(); },);And this way, only my authenticated users that have read access can see the requested document !

Case closed 
